<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sima Qian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 145–c. 86 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sima Qian (c. 145–c. 86 BCE) was a historian of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> who completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> (Shiji), a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the legendary sage kings to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. Castrated as punishment after defending a disgraced general, he chose to live on to finish the work. Its arrangement of annals, treatises and biographies became the model for China's official dynastic histories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sima-qian.jpg" alt="Sima Qian" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_maqian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
